Read Jira ticket ABC-123 using the jira MCP server — it contains the full
spec and acceptance criteria.

Clone the repo, removing any leftover from a previous run first:
`rm -rf /workspace/example-service && gh repo clone your-org/example-service /workspace/example-service`
(replace the owner/repo). cd into it — the clone leaves you on the repo's
default branch, whatever it is called.

Record the base branch for later: `BASE=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref origin/HEAD)`
(prints e.g. origin/main or origin/develop). If this repo merges features
somewhere other than its default branch, set BASE to that instead.

Create a feature branch named after the ticket (e.g. abc-123-<short-slug>)
and implement the ticket according to its acceptance criteria. Commit in
small increments as you work — an interrupted run keeps whatever is
committed, and loses whatever is not.

Immediately before pushing, collapse those increments into a single
commit: `git reset --soft "$BASE"`, then one commit whose message
covers the whole change and references ABC-123. One commit per PR.

Push the branch and open a Pull Request with `gh pr create --base "${BASE#origin/}"`,
summarizing what was implemented and how it maps to the ticket's
acceptance criteria. Reference ABC-123 in the PR body.

Do NOT merge the PR, and do NOT review it or leave review comments on it —
review is a human's job. Stop once the PR is open.
